WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.476
Lesson seven.

00:00:01.476 --> 00:00:04.694
The champion is nine hundred and thirty two numbers.

00:00:04.694 --> 00:00:05.981
What do they do?

00:00:05.981 --> 00:00:08.748
Let's open the network while it walks.

00:00:09.348 --> 00:00:14.375
Each row is one of the thirty two hidden neurons, and time runs to the right.

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:17.036
Blue is positive, orange is negative.

00:00:17.036 --> 00:00:21.299
At the bottom, you can see when each foot is on the ground.

00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:23.931
Look for repeating stripes.

00:00:23.931 --> 00:00:26.833
Many neurons switch in step with the feet.

00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:32.696
And about fifty seven percent of the time, a neuron sits at its limit, fully on or fully off.

00:00:32.696 --> 00:00:35.260
Many of them act like switches.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:41.672
Now cut the walk into strides, from one touchdown of the first foot to the next, and average.

00:00:41.672 --> 00:00:46.735
A stride takes about fifteen steps, which is roughly three hundred milliseconds.

00:00:46.735 --> 00:00:48.934
It's a quick, short gait.

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:53.353
The network has no memory, but it still produces a rhythm.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:56.940
The rhythm lives in the loop between body and network.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.216
The legs move, their angles feed back as inputs, and the next command follows.

00:01:02.816 --> 00:01:04.522
Which neurons matter?

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:09.816
To find out, freeze one at its average value and let the walker try twenty tracks.

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:11.998
This is called ablation.

00:01:12.598 --> 00:01:14.638
Most neurons barely matter.

00:01:14.638 --> 00:01:20.057
But freeze neuron twenty five, and the score drops by three hundred and fifty points.

00:01:20.057 --> 00:01:23.504
The walker falls on eighteen of twenty tracks.

00:01:24.104 --> 00:01:30.370
In this network, nine of the thirty two neurons each cost more than fifty points when frozen.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:33.834
The rest is spare capacity, or fine tuning.

00:01:34.434 --> 00:01:37.271
The same test works for the senses.

00:01:37.271 --> 00:01:42.518
The most important ones are hip 1 angle, knee 1 angle, hip 1 speed.

00:01:43.118 --> 00:01:46.934
And the lidar, the rays that look ahead, barely matters.

00:01:46.934 --> 00:01:50.630
This walker walks by feel, reacting to its own joints.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:56.296
That's enough on these gentle tracks, but it's a likely weakness on unusual bumps.

00:01:56.896 --> 00:02:04.615
Next, the final test: a hundred tracks it has never seen, and the comparison with my other walkers.
